 #:seon.task{:name "a-one-line-change-resends-the-whole-definition"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent "There is no REPL entrance to change part of an existing definition: to add one map entry to `seon.render.value/tree` the agent resent the whole 38-line defn (about 1,200 tokens) each attempt, and the whole 45-line mcp-get-value (about 1,300 tokens) to change one contract keyword, where a file edit sends one line. my.edit edits files, not program entities. An entrance that takes the stored declaration, applies a form-level replacement and evaluates the result as the defn would make the REPL cheaper than the file for the common edit."
             :must-read #{my.program/history my.edit/form!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "propose-refusal-names-no-next-step"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(`my.program/propose-merge!` {}) on an unassigned branch answers the bare string \"seon.task is refused: unassigned.\" (the declared renderer shows only the message): no offending branch/agent, no fix naming who assigns (root's `seon.task/assign!`) or how a file task in resources/seon/tasks becomes assignable. An agent also has no my.* entrance to create its own branch (MCP's branch tool has none for Seon agents). The refusal names its assignment query and the call that fixes it."
             :must-read #{my.program/propose-merge! seon.task/assignment seon.task/assign!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-merged-change-reaching-the-files-is-unproven"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The last unproven step of the dogfood path: an agent's merged change reaches the files through write-back (`my.program/write-back!`). Done when a live branch change is proposed, merged and written back, the file diff equals the change, and the run is recorded as evidence (tmp/repl-audit/change.md lists the prior gaps)."
             :must-read #{my.program/prepare-merge! my.program/write-back!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/repl-audit--change.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "history-shows-the-source-twice-and-no-commit-id"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/history` shows a definition's source twice per event and the write-back transaction carries no commit id. Done when each history event names the commit id recorded on its write-back transaction and shows a diff render instead of the second source copy."
             :must-read #{my.program/declaration-diff my.program/history}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/repl-audit--change.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "ns-unmap-and-remove-ns-cost-quarter-thousand-tokens-and-most-of-a-second"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/ns-unmap!` and remove-ns! add about 250 tokens of constant prose to every reply and take 0.6-0.8 s. Done when a reply is the value and one line, and the call is under 300 ms, timed against the same probe on the parent commit."
             :must-read #{my.program/ns-unmap! my.program/remove-ns!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/repl-audit--change.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-merge-prepare-costs-three-seconds-and-400k-calls"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The merge path: `my.program/prepare-merge!` takes 2.6-3.1 s and about 400k calls regardless of change size. The cold-projection hypothesis was falsified (800a94abb); it needs a sampled profile (Astra after a bow-out), then the responsible armed functions named and fixed. Done when a small merge prepares under one second, timed on parent and change."
             :must-read #{my.program/prepare-merge! seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "alias-and-require-as-retarget-silently"
  :kind :function
  :intent "In SCI, (require '[clojure.set :as x]) after x -> clojure.string returns nil and retargets the alias; so does (alias 'x 'clojure.walk) (sci-alias is a bare assoc-in, namespaces.cljc:478-486). Clojure refuses a different target (Namespace.java:257-258, \"Alias x already exists in namespace .., aliasing ..\"). The silent retarget makes stored definitions position-dependent and is the source of the digest refusals in the alias design probes. Correct: alias and require :as with a different target refuse as Clojure does, ns-unalias! being the guarded path (SCI runs ns-unalias itself); a refer replacement stays legal (a warning in Clojure). Related issue docs/seon/issues/reload-refuses-an-alias-retargeted-by-the-new-source.md. Proof: evaluating (alias 'x 'clojure.set) after x -> clojure.string answers a refusal error and leaves the alias unchanged."
  :must-read #{my.program/ns-unalias!}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md" "docs/seon/issues/reload-refuses-an-alias-retargeted-by-the-new-source.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "merge-tools-throw-instead-of-returning"
             :kind :function
             :intent "propose-merge! and prepare-merge! are returning tools but throw: propose-merge! on a branch whose contract names an agent-registered schema throws :malli.core/invalid-schema out of `seon.test/run` (seon.test/check-fails-on-agent-registered-schemas), and prepare-merge! of a test-only new namespace throws entity-id/missing. Done when each returns its error map naming the failing proof or row, regressions through MCP SCI."
             :must-read #{my.program/propose-merge! my.program/prepare-merge!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "root-cannot-diff-a-proposal"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/diff` compares only my own branch with my cluster head; root reviewing a proposal has no entrance to diff the proposal's branch against the head it would merge onto. The dogfood needed JVM mode, seon.operator.runtime/running-instances for the store, and `seon.cluster.source/compare-branch` (174 ms). Done when (`my.program/diff` {`:seon.task/merge` m}) answers the merge diff value (docs/seon/specs/merge.md §2) from root's context."
             :must-read #{my.program/diff seon.cluster.source/compare-branch}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "accept-cannot-retire-a-hosted-proposers-branch"
             :kind :function
             :intent "accept-merge!'s detached retire of the proposing branch fails while the proposer's hosted agent holds a connection ('branch :agent-099e88cb509c still has a connection in this process'), recorded as an open fault produced by `my.program/accept-merge!` (2026-10-08); the branch stays in the roster. Done when accepting a hosted agent's proposal releases its acquisition and retires its branch, regression through MCP SCI."
             :must-read #{my.program/accept-merge! my.agent/retire-branch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "breaks-on-a-namespace-subject-takes-five-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/breaks` on the namespace subject seon.turn took 5542 ms and 5876 ms on default's JVM, against 183 ms for the function subject `seon.render.value/render-ai`, so the test schema-and-namespace-reads-keep-different-connections-distinct exceeds its 5000 ms bound. Find what the namespace branch of breaks is proportional to (owned declarations, gate names, requiring namespaces) and make it sub-second; done when that test passes inside its bound."
             :must-read #{my.program/breaks seon.fn/gate-sets seon.fn/gating-tests}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/merge.md" "docs/seon/schedule/cards/PB-15.md"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "write-branch-writes-files-the-lane-has-not-claimed"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/write-branch!` (RF-1, 24e8cc2df) writes a branch's declarations into whatever files hold them, including files the lane has not claimed, after which bin/lane claim refuses those files as someone else's uncommitted change. Correct: before writing, it answers the paths it will write and refuses, as data naming them, any path the lane has not claimed. Proof: a my.program-test deftest (to be written) whose branch touches an unclaimed file asserts the refusal names that path and no file changed."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/RF-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "diff-answers-empty-after-a-branch-write-back"
             :kind :function
             :intent "After a branch write-back of `my.turn/wait` through `my.program/write-branch!` (RF-1, 24e8cc2df), `my.program/diff` on that branch answered every section empty. Cause unknown: either the write-back moved the branch's comparison base to its own change (expected) or diff lost a real change (a defect). Done when a REPL reproduction names which, and a regression asserts the diff a write-back should leave. Proof: a my.program-test deftest (to be written)."
             :must-read #{my.program/diff my.program/write-branch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/RF-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "write-branch-in-the-defn-request-answers-a-merge-changed-nothing"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/write-branch!` evaluated in the same eval request as the `defn` it should write reads the branch before the recording transaction and answers `:no-replacement` \"The accepted merge changed no declaration.\", naming a merge the agent never made. Done when it answers in write-back terms (nothing on the branch differs from its head yet; evaluate it in the next eval request), as `my.test/check` answers `:my.test/after-recording`."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! seon.cluster.source/write-back!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/dogfood/pilot.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-proof-test-fails-from-concurrent-clusters-drift"
             :kind :function
             :intent "my.program-test/past-reach-is-advisory-and-source-history-survives-retraction fails from worker-global drift caused by other lanes' running agents (running-agent-...) (PB-15 dogfood run, lane dogfood-pb15, default, 2026-10-08): it is not isolated from concurrent clusters. Done when it passes while other agents run, asserting only its own world."
             :must-read #{my.program-test/past-reach-is-advisory-and-source-history-survives-retraction}}
 #:seon.task{:name "remove-ns-allocates-two-gigabytes"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(my.program/remove-ns! 'tq.probe) allocated 2,061 MB in 839 ms for a tiny namespace (lane assign-reply). Done when the cost follows the namespace's own definitions."
             :must-read #{my.program/remove-ns!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-root-repl-proof-cannot-reach-its-file"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Root gives a real backlog task its red proof by a deftest at its REPL on main (a proof must be at the home before seon.task/assign! accepts it), but nothing writes that deftest to its file: bin/lane evidence for an agent's branch called test/my/program_test.clj unanalyzable once the deftest was appended by hand (it used clojure.string/includes? without a require), and differing (namespace row and test digest) after adding the require (fanout run, default e1a71b7c8, 2026-10-08), so each proof landed through a separate 55 s throwaway check (e39453e05). Done when root writes its own main definitions back with the same write-back and the proof file commits with the agent's change by branch evidence."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! seon.cluster.branch-evidence/branch-evidence seon.task/assign!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "breaks-of-a-widely-used-function-allocates-a-gigabyte"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(my.program/breaks {:seon.program/subject 'seon.db/pull}) at an SCI agent's REPL allocated 1,200 MB in 622 ms (:large-allocation finding; context corpus row 15, default 56f176efc, 2026-10-09); at the JVM REPL the same read plus seon.fn/gating-tests and its render took 410 ms over 343,906 armed calls and 29,184 reads, for 610 call sites and 2,297 gating tests. Its AI render (my.program/render-breakage-ai) is 1 ms. Done when the read's allocation and reads follow its callers and gating set, measured with seon.profile/measure."
             :must-read #{my.program/breaks seon.fn/gating-tests}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-clojure-style-ns-unmap-is-refused-as-a-missing-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(`my.program/ns-unmap!` 'seon.help-test 'help-teaches-how-a-deftest-names-its-task), the two-argument call of `clojure.core/ns-unmap`, answered \"refused argument 0 `context`: expected :my.program/context (a map), got a symbol seon.help-test\" with the world's whole context schema as its problem (fanout-3 run, default 324461975, 2026-10-09); the call that works takes one qualified symbol. Done when a two-symbol call either unmaps that namespace's name as Clojure does or is refused as an arity error naming the one-symbol form. Reconfirmed 2026-10-10 (lane chain-frictions): AGENTS.md (\"Agents change the program in REPL terms\") names `my.program/ns-unmap!` and `my.program/remove-ns!` without their arguments, and loop-prove (86b7cdbbe) called `ns-unmap!` with a symbol as that prose suggests and was refused for a missing context map. Done also when the AGENTS.md line names the call that works."
             :must-read #{my.program/ns-unmap!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-repl-alias-stays-on-the-namespace-row-after-ns-unalias"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Agent fan4-empty (fanout-4, default 97f2b2ac3, 2026-10-09) added the alias string for clojure.string in seon.error at its REPL; the alias joined the stored seon.error namespace row (its definition digest changed), and (`clojure.core/ns-unalias` 'seon.error 'string) left the row unchanged, so `my.program/diff` kept listing the namespace as changed and `my.program/write-branch!` refused the file. Done when an alias removed with ns-unalias leaves the namespace row as it was, or the diff names the alias as the change and offers the form that removes it."
             :must-read #{my.program/diff my.program/write-branch!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "write-branch-in-the-running-agent-harness-compares-with-a-home-branch-the-agent-did-not-fork-from"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Under `seon.test-support/with-running-agent`, an agent with its own branch forks from the member's connection, but `my.program/write-branch!` compares with the cluster's home branch (`seon.cluster.branch/cluster-branch`), which lacks fixture rows transacted on the member: every fixture declaration, the harness agent's namespace and user read as added and write-branch! refuses :one-file (lane ns-form, 2026-10-09). So no MCP regression can drive write-branch! end to end; `seon.turn.batch-test/a-repl-require-is-written-back-in-the-ns-form-and-reindexes-equal` calls `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` between the fork point and the branch head instead. Done when the harness's member branch is the head write-branch! compares with (or the comparison takes the branch's fork parent), and that test calls write-branch!."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! seon.test-support/with-running-agent seon.cluster.source/write-back!}}
#:seon.task{:name "write-branch-of-one-function-allocates-a-gigabyte"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`my.program/write-branch!` of one docstring change in src/seon/error.clj (~3,000 lines) allocated 1,032 MB in 517 ms (lane wb-scalar, 2026-10-09): `seon.cluster.source/proven-file` 391 MB / 199 ms (`seon.fn.analyzer/invoke-kondo` re-analyzing the whole file, 129 query reads), `seon.cluster.source/compare-branch` 185 MB / 78 ms (8 reads), ~450 MB unattributed in the SCI evaluation path. Expected: proportional to the changed forms, tens of MB."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/write-back! my.program/write-branch!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-map-argument-proof-errors-reading-its-reply"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`(my.program/diff {})` answers `{:added [], :changed [], :removed []}` like `(my.program/diff)` through MCP on default (2026-10-09), yet its proof `my.program-test/a-map-argument-lacking-world-members-is-filled-from-the-world` errors at main: inside `seon.test-support/with-running-agent`, reading the first reply (`(my.program/diff)`) with `seon.test-support/reply` throws `Invalid token: /Users/sean/src/seon/data/store` (test report e50e75cc6ba9), so some content item of that reply is not data: a bare store path. Done when every content item of that eval request reads back as data and the proof is green, so `my.program/diff-with-a-map-argument-is-refused-as-a-context` can retire."
             :must-read #{my.program/diff seon.test-support/mcp-reply seon.test-support/with-running-agent}}
 #:seon.task{:name "write-branch-with-a-relative-root-writes-into-the-running-jvms-archive"
             :kind :function
             :intent "DATA-SAFETY. `(my.program/write-branch! {:seon.fn/root \".\"})` at the live REPL wrote the branch's files into the running JVM's own archive (data/source/<sha>/test/...) instead of the checkout (lane loop-prove, 86b7cdbbe, 2026-10-10). Cause confirmed: `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` installs with (.getPath (io/file root path)) and `:seon.fn/root` is any string, so \".\" resolves against the JVM's working directory, and a Seon JVM runs from its committed archive. Read-only probe on default caba650fa (agent chain-frictions-probe, mode jvm): (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") answers \"/Users/sean/src/seon/data/source/caba650fadb50028456796bba08f166ed1909364\", (.getPath (java.io.File. \".\" \"test/x.clj\")) \"./test/x.clj\" resolves into it, and (seon.fs/source-directory) is the same archive, so the JVM names no checkout at all. Done when `:seon.fn/root` is refused unless it is an absolute path to an existing directory that is not under data/source (and not the JVM's archive), the refusal naming the checkout and why, asserted on the refusal data with no file written; and an agent has a documented read that answers the checkout (or the docstring example says to pass the absolute path). The same check covers `my.program/write-back!` and `my.program/prepare-merge!`."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! seon.cluster.source/write-back! seon.fs/source-directory}}
 #:seon.task{:name "write-branch-identities-take-tuples-and-the-docs-do-not-say"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`:seon.program/identities` of `my.program/write-branch!` and `my.test/check` is `:seon.program/identity-set` (a set of `:seon.program/declaration-identity`, resources/seon/schemas/seon.program.edn), and lane loop-prove (86b7cdbbe, 2026-10-10) found it takes tuples where its prose and `my.program/diff` sections show symbols; the refusal showed a generated example rather than a valid one. Done when the docstring example of `my.program/write-branch!` passes a real identity copied from `my.program/diff`, and a symbol where a tuple is expected is refused naming the tuple shape."
             :must-read #{my.program/write-branch! my.program/diff}}]
